An Availability Contract records nine fields: permitted claim q q ; input boundary B B ;
source-to-completion-proxy rule G G ; allowance ℓ \ell ; checkpoint record K K ,
including its identity, knowledge boundary, and point-in-time availability;
inference-completion evidence M M ; price-anchor rule 𝒜 \mathcal{A} ; endpoint E E ; and sample rule 𝒮 \mathcal{S} . Label start applies 𝒜 \mathcal{A} to G ⁡ ( B ) + ℓ G(B)+\ell under the stated market calendar. The contract specifies a
reproducible window whose interpretation follows the recorded evidence for G G , K K , and M M : a proxy-aligned
association, a verified post-input evaluation, or a deployment claim.

